# androidTest/.../staggeritem/StaggerItemUiTest.kt                137 lines  (per-state UI + a11y label test)

# ── Web-source parity (web/src/components/motion/StaggerItem.tsx) ───────────────────────────────────────────
# The web StaggerItem reads useMotionPreference(350) and defines two framer-motion variants: hidden
# (reduce ? {opacity:1,y:0} : {opacity:0,y:15}) and show ({opacity:1,y:0, transition:{duration: durationMs/1000}}).
# A single child fades up from 15 px below over 350 ms; under reduced motion hidden collapses to the final frame so
# the child appears in place with no fade/slide. The inter-sibling delay is owned by the parent StaggerContainer's
# staggerChildren; on native there is no parent-orchestrated clock, so the per-item delay is derived from the
# child ordinal by the tested staggerDelayMs (ADR-005). Native maps the web 350 ms / 15 px to the P1/S9 motion
# tokens (MotionDefaults.ITEM_MS / SLIDE_DP) per "use platform tokens, do not port web literals". The surface
# composes the component-library motion atom (io.teslasync.android.components.motion.StaggerItem) so the surface
# and the atom can never drift on what the fade + slide + delay mean; the pure staggerItemPlan projection mirrors
# that same math off-device. No i18n strings (the web source renders no text); no HTTP (web parity).

# ── States reproduced (the web source has no async query, so the generic loading/error/stale/offline states do
#    not apply — documented in StaggerItemModel.kt with the honesty rationale; the REAL states are reproduced) ──
# animated entrance (motion enabled: fade + slide up from the offset)   — staggerItemPlan(reduce=false) + UI test
# collapsed immediate frame (reduced motion: web hidden = show)         — staggerItemPlan(reduce=true)  + UI test
# first-item zero-delay entrance (no preceding siblings)                — staggerItemPlan(index=0)
# transparent pass-through (child renders verbatim, semantics intact)   — UI a11y reachability test

# ── Unit test evidence (:android:testReleaseUnitTest) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# StaggerItemModelTest        : tests=7 failures=0 errors=0 skipped=0  (entrance per-state snapshot: animated /
#                               reduced-motion / first-item / negative-index clamp / negative-slide clamp /
#                               reduced-duration collapse / web-parity defaults)
# StaggerItemDiagnosticsTest  : tests=2 failures=0 errors=0 skipped=0  (PII-safe view.opened slug contract)

# ── On-device UI + a11y test (src/androidTest/.../staggeritem/StaggerItemUiTest.kt) ─────────────────────────
# Ships a connectedAndroidTest covering every state (child final state under reduced motion, a row of
# hand-staggered children, the surface test tag, the one-shot view.opened diagnostic, and child-label
# accessibility reachability). It is ktlint-clean (runKtlintCheckOverAndroidTestSourceSet executed + passed in
# STEP 3) and compiles error-free: when :android:compileDebugAndroidTestKotlin was run as an extra check (NOT a
# gate step — the gate is assembleRelease + testReleaseUnitTest + ktlintCheck + detekt), the StaggerItem UI test
# produced NO compiler error (grep for "staggeritem" over the compiler output = 0 matches; total error lines
# dropped 66 -> 65 after fixing this surface's only error). The source-set task still fails to compile AS A WHOLE
# due to PRE-EXISTING errors in ~39 unrelated sibling UI tests (e.g. routedisplay/scorebadge/treeselect/
# notionsidebar and the dashboard widgets mis-import androidx.compose.ui.test.assertExists / assertDoesNotExist /
# onAllNodes, which are absent from this classpath). Those are outside this artifact's allowed files and are left
# untouched; this surface's UI test deliberately uses only the resolvable assertIsDisplayed/onNodeWithText/
# onNodeWithTag API so it is the one test in its cohort that compiles cleanly.

